2. Irony: Socrates often used irony to challenge the beliefs of his interlocutors. He would ask seemingly naive or absurd questions, forcing his interlocutors to examine their own beliefs more closely.
3. Cross-Examination: Socrates was known for his cross-examination of his interlocutors, in which he would ask a series of questions that led to contradictions or inconsistencies in their beliefs.
4. Analogy: Socrates used analogies to challenge the beliefs of his interlocutors. He would compare their beliefs to other situations or ideas, revealing similarities or differences that might not have been apparent before.
5. Refutation: Socrates often used refutation to challenge the beliefs of his interlocutors. He would argue against their beliefs, showing that they were illogical or inconsistent, in order to stimulate critical thinking and to reveal the flaws in their arguments.
Overall, Socrates employed a variety of methods to challenge the prevailing thoughts of his time, encouraging critical thinking and questioning of traditional beliefs and ideas
